# Narration for "Rate code, burst code: how one hub puts the whole brain on a 2-hertz clock". Kokoro am_michael, speed 1.1.
# Keys: intro, s1_A..C, s2_A..E, s3_A..D, outro.
N = {}
N["intro"] = """How does a neuron tell the rest of the brain that something happened? Normally, with its rate. This is the story of what happens to that code when the cell is hyperpolarized and its H-C-N-one channels wake up — and why, I think, that is how a sedated brain ends up running on a single two-hertz clock. Three steps: the rate code, the burst code, and the spread."""

N["s1_A"] = """Here is an ordinary cortical neuron, as a two-dimensional model: membrane voltage left to right, potassium gate bottom to top. The input current, I, is the knob. At its resting input the cell ticks along at about ten spikes a second. Each lap of the magenta loop is one spike. Everything here is slowed ten times, so one lap a second on screen is ten hertz."""
N["s1_B"] = """Now an event arrives — a sound, a touch, a flash. The input steps up for a third of a second. The loop tightens, and the cell runs it faster: fifty, sixty spikes a second. When the input drops back, so does the rate. A weaker event, a smaller step: thirty hertz. The code is graded — more stimulus, more spikes per second. A dial, not a switch — and the cells downstream read the dial by counting."""
N["s1_C"] = """Notice what never happens: the cell never goes silent. Ten hertz is its floor. Every moment is reported, with the volume turned up for events."""

N["s2_A"] = """Now hyperpolarize the cell. Cut the glutamate it receives — T-H-C does this through presynaptic C-B-one receptors; ketamine does it by blocking N-M-D-A receptors — and the membrane sags to about minus sixty millivolts. Two things change. The resting input is now below threshold, so left alone the cell is off: no spikes at all. And below minus sixty, H-C-N-one channels begin to open. They carry a slow inward current, I-h, that grows while the cell is hyperpolarized and shrinks while it is depolarized."""
N["s2_B"] = """Watch the gate, h, on the right. While the cell is silent, h creeps up, I-h grows, the teal nullcline lifts, and the resting state slides toward the saddle. When they touch — the fold — rest disappears, and the state falls onto the spiking loop. This loop is fast: seventy to eighty spikes a second, because this cell's potassium gate is quick. But spiking depolarizes the cell, so now h drains. The nullcline sinks, the loop shrinks toward the saddle, and at the homoclinic the cell drops off — back to rest, silent. Then h creeps up again. Burst, silence, burst, silence: about twice a second."""
N["s2_C"] = """In three dimensions it is one closed loop. Up the resting branch as h accumulates; over the fold onto the tube of cycles; down the tube as h drains; off the tube at the homoclinic. Thalamic relay cells do the same dance with T-type calcium channels instead of H-C-N-one: a channel that recovers under hyperpolarization and fires a low-threshold spike crowned with a burst. Same geometry, different slow variable."""
N["s2_D"] = """Now send in events. Through a sedated synapse they arrive small and brief: a shove, not a push. A shove late in the silent phase tips the cell over the fold early, and out comes the whole burst, at eighty hertz — the same burst whether the shove was strong or weak. A shove in the middle of a burst changes nothing. And a shove right after a burst, before h has recovered, gets a stunted burst or none at all."""
N["s2_E"] = """So the graded dial is gone. What is left is a switch — eighty hertz, or off — and a clock that decides when the switch may be thrown."""

N["s3_A"] = """Why doesn't this stay in one cell? Layer five of the retrosplenial cortex is dense with H-C-N-one. In the mice of Vesuna and colleagues, ketamine produced exactly this rhythm there — one to three hertz in layer five — it needed H-C-N-one, and playing it back optogenetically was enough to produce dissociation-like behaviour. In their mice the rhythm stayed coupled to the thalamus and uncoupled from most of the rest of the brain. My claim, from my own sixteen-channel E-E-G under heavier sedation, is that the pulse does not stay home: the whole cap goes to about two a second. Here is how that could work."""
N["s3_B"] = """Thirty retrosplenial cells, each a burster, each on its own clock, started at random phases. Now let them talk — weak mutual excitation. A burst in a few of them shoves the others over the fold, and within a handful of cycles they are one volley: thirty cells at eighty hertz, all at once. That is a massive, synchronous input to everything they project to."""
N["s3_C"] = """The next stage — posterior cingulate, medial prefrontal, the hippocampal formation, the rest of the default mode network — is sitting in the same bath. Hyperpolarized, a little below threshold, it cannot carry a graded rate any more. But an eighty-hertz volley it cannot ignore: the population is kicked over threshold together, fires a volley of its own, and falls silent. One stage further out, the same. Each relay squeezes the code flatter: what arrives as nuance leaves as a yes or a no, and every yes lands on the same beat."""
N["s3_D"] = """Within a second or two, every population in the chain is pulsing at two hertz — on, off, on, off — with a small delay outward from the retrosplenial source. Summed, that is a two-hertz wave. It is what the E-E-G sees. And I think it is what the mind sees too: a world that arrives in frames, about two a second."""

N["outro"] = """So. Awake, an event is a change of rate — ten to sixty hertz, graded and continuous. Hyperpolarized, with H-C-N-one or T-channels in charge, an event is a burst — eighty hertz or nothing — and the bursts are gated by a two-hertz clock. And because the retrosplenial hub flips first and shouts loudest, its clock propagates outward, stage by stage, until it is the brain's clock. Model and code are in the description."""
